# Routing only. No credential: qits-artifacts requires and reads none, in either direction, and an
# install needs nothing at all — only `npm publish` has a client-side pre-flight, and nothing here
# publishes.
#
# localhost:8081 is the local platform's host-published qits-artifacts port, and it is the address
# for the one consumer this file exists for: a developer on the deployment host, dialling it
# directly inside the trusted surface. Nothing else reads it — every build runs in a container on
# qits-net, where this address does not exist, and takes the qits-net alias from the environment.
#
# CI overrides this file's effect rather than editing it: the pipeline sets npm_config_registry /
# npm_config_@qits:registry from $QITS_NPM_PROXY_URL / $QITS_NPM_REGISTRY_URL — the qits-net
# aliases (qits-artifacts:8080) — and environment outranks both a project and a user .npmrc.
#
# The lockfile is a separate problem, and it is npm's: npm records an absolute `resolved` URL per
# package, so package-lock.json spells *this* address ~700 times and is not host-neutral the way
# pnpm-lock.yaml was. .config/qits/ci-post-receive.yml retargets those origins before `npm ci`,
# and the comment there explains why npm's own `replace-registry-host` cannot do it.
registry=http://localhost:8081/artifacts/npm/npmjs/
@qits:registry=http://localhost:8081/artifacts/npm/npm/
